1
00:00:00,417 --> 00:00:03,002
<i>- Previously on "The Ark"...</i>

2
00:00:03,003 --> 00:00:05,003
- Here we are again.
- Is he even alive?

3
00:00:05,004 --> 00:00:07,965
- [grunting]
- Get him free. Now!

4
00:00:07,966 --> 00:00:10,008
- Easy.
- [gasps]

5
00:00:10,009 --> 00:00:11,051
- Got a visual ahead.

6
00:00:11,052 --> 00:00:12,261
<i>- Guys, I am telling you,</i>

7
00:00:12,262 --> 00:00:13,887
it seemed like Doc was there, right?

8
00:00:13,888 --> 00:00:15,764
And she was the one
who fixed me up.

9
00:00:15,765 --> 00:00:17,599
- I think it's Admiral Hammond.

10
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,227
- Who is she?
- I'm not sure yet.

11
00:00:20,228 --> 00:00:22,146
- Mr. Strickland,
is that your sword?

12
00:00:22,147 --> 00:00:23,314
Did you do this?

13
00:00:23,315 --> 00:00:24,481
- I barely knew
Admiral Hammond,

14
00:00:24,482 --> 00:00:26,191
let alone had a reason
to kill him.

15
00:00:26,192 --> 00:00:29,862
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

16
00:00:29,863 --> 00:00:32,906
<i>[curious music]</i>

17
00:00:32,907 --> 00:00:35,200
[door whooshes]

18
00:00:35,201 --> 00:00:38,871
- So what couldn't wait
till I finished my lunch?

19
00:00:38,872 --> 00:00:41,165
- I've got the rest
of the results of our study.

20
00:00:41,166 --> 00:00:42,458
It's kind of blowing my mind.

21
00:00:42,459 --> 00:00:44,835
The grass is <i>Cynodon dactylon.</i>

22
00:00:44,836 --> 00:00:46,337
- Bermuda grass.

23
00:00:46,338 --> 00:00:49,214
- The trees are poplar,
field maple, and scarlet oak.

24
00:00:49,215 --> 00:00:51,592
- Mm-hmm. And the bird?

25
00:00:51,593 --> 00:00:52,885
<i>- Turdus merula.</i>

26
00:00:52,886 --> 00:00:54,219
- Eurasian blackbird.

27
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:55,804
They're really common on Earth.

28
00:00:55,805 --> 00:00:57,681
- All of it's common on Earth.
- No kidding.

29
00:00:57,682 --> 00:00:59,183
- But that's crazy.

30
00:00:59,184 --> 00:01:00,309
Why would they all be
on a planet

31
00:01:00,310 --> 00:01:01,727
light-years away from Earth?

32
00:01:01,728 --> 00:01:02,978
- They wouldn't.

33
00:01:02,979 --> 00:01:04,688
You've got it wrong.

34
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:06,732
Run the data again.

35
00:01:06,733 --> 00:01:08,025
- I've run it three times.

36
00:01:08,026 --> 00:01:09,860
It's accurate.

37
00:01:09,861 --> 00:01:11,779
[device beeping]

38
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:14,073
[chimes]

39
00:01:14,074 --> 00:01:16,367
- Which means
the same plants and animals

40
00:01:16,368 --> 00:01:18,911
that evolved on Earth
evolved here.

41
00:01:18,912 --> 00:01:21,705
Parallel evolution.
The odds of that are like nil.

42
00:01:21,706 --> 00:01:23,582
- They're not like nil.

43
00:01:23,583 --> 00:01:25,376
They are nil.

44
00:01:25,377 --> 00:01:27,378
- Maybe the people
in those caves know something

45
00:01:27,379 --> 00:01:29,254
that can help us
figure this out.

46
00:01:29,255 --> 00:01:31,590
- The people that knocked
you out and kidnapped you?

47
00:01:31,591 --> 00:01:33,092
Those people?

48
00:01:33,093 --> 00:01:36,011
- Well, now, the idea that
they're humans who evolved here

49
00:01:36,012 --> 00:01:37,930
feels less unlikely.

50
00:01:37,931 --> 00:01:40,265
- They were trying to tell me
and Kelly something.

51
00:01:40,266 --> 00:01:41,850
I'm gonna go see if I can
get back in there,

52
00:01:41,851 --> 00:01:42,976
try to communicate with them.

53
00:01:42,977 --> 00:01:44,228
- Then I'm going with you.

54
00:01:44,229 --> 00:01:45,479
- I think you're both
out of your minds.

55
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:47,189
But if you insist on going,
I suggest you take

56
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:48,774
several large men with guns.

57
00:01:48,775 --> 00:01:52,027
<i>[tense music]</i>

58
00:01:52,028 --> 00:01:57,409
<i>♪ ♪</i>

59
00:02:03,790 --> 00:02:05,750
- Mind if I join you?

60
00:02:08,670 --> 00:02:10,045
- You're not real.

61
00:02:10,046 --> 00:02:11,755
- Yes, I am.

62
00:02:11,756 --> 00:02:14,091
- No, no, no, no, no, no.

63
00:02:14,092 --> 00:02:15,676
You can't be.

64
00:02:15,677 --> 00:02:17,177
You're in my head.

65
00:02:17,178 --> 00:02:19,972
- You're the only one
who can perceive me.

66
00:02:19,973 --> 00:02:21,807
You can see me.

67
00:02:21,808 --> 00:02:24,977
You can hear me.

68
00:02:24,978 --> 00:02:28,731
- Okay, okay.
[laughs nervously]

69
00:02:28,732 --> 00:02:32,234
Yes, well, um--so this--

70
00:02:32,235 --> 00:02:33,861
this has got something
to do with that thing

71
00:02:33,862 --> 00:02:35,988
that I took out from behind
my ear back in the caves,

72
00:02:35,989 --> 00:02:37,614
doesn't it?

73
00:02:37,615 --> 00:02:41,076
Somehow that thing--that thing
put you in my head, didn't it?

74
00:02:41,077 --> 00:02:42,119
- See what I mean?

75
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:44,121
You're so smart.

76
00:02:44,122 --> 00:02:45,873
- So that's a yes, is it?

77
00:02:45,874 --> 00:02:48,083
- I saw an opportunity when
I detected the dormant chip

78
00:02:48,084 --> 00:02:49,835
in your head, so I took it.

79
00:02:49,836 --> 00:02:52,880
- Maddox's frickin'
mind-control chip.

80
00:02:52,881 --> 00:02:56,133
I knew--I knew I should've made
Kabir take it out.

81
00:02:56,134 --> 00:02:57,634
- Good thing she didn't.
- But, no, no.

82
00:02:57,635 --> 00:02:59,136
She said,
don't worry about it, James.

83
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:00,637
There's no--no risk
involved, James.

84
00:03:00,638 --> 00:03:04,183
Well, this looks like
a big risk to me, Sanji!

85
00:03:04,184 --> 00:03:06,310
- [voice reverberates]
Calm down, buddy.

86
00:03:06,311 --> 00:03:07,978
- Wait a second.

87
00:03:07,979 --> 00:03:10,981
That thing, it was behind
Eva, Garnet's, Kimi's,

88
00:03:10,982 --> 00:03:13,108
and Sanji's ears as well?

89
00:03:13,109 --> 00:03:14,526
But not Ian's, right?

90
00:03:14,527 --> 00:03:18,155
Because Ian, he doesn't have
a chip in his head.

91
00:03:18,156 --> 00:03:20,240
- So smart.

92
00:03:20,241 --> 00:03:21,450
- So what, are you, uh--
you're in all

93
00:03:21,451 --> 00:03:23,077
of their heads as well now,
are you?

94
00:03:23,078 --> 00:03:24,244
- I tried.

95
00:03:24,245 --> 00:03:27,623
But they weren't
attached long enough.

96
00:03:27,624 --> 00:03:29,249
I like you.

97
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:31,293
You'll be the perfect partner.

98
00:03:31,294 --> 00:03:32,711
- Partner?

99
00:03:32,712 --> 00:03:34,129
Partner for what?

100
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:36,048
- Oh, it's really quite simple.

101
00:03:36,049 --> 00:03:39,718
Part of me is on Homebase,
but that part is sick.

102
00:03:39,719 --> 00:03:43,138
So it sent a message to part
of me on Ross asking for help.

103
00:03:43,139 --> 00:03:44,390
- Okay.

104
00:03:44,391 --> 00:03:46,141
You sound crazier
than I'm feeling.

105
00:03:46,142 --> 00:03:47,768
- How is that crazy?

106
00:03:47,769 --> 00:03:49,436
On Ross, I fixed you

107
00:03:49,437 --> 00:03:52,231
so you could go back
to Homebase and fix me.

108
00:03:52,232 --> 00:03:53,650
- Why?

109
00:03:55,193 --> 00:03:57,403
- Because by saving me,

110
00:03:57,404 --> 00:03:59,113
you'll save the people
you care about.

111
00:03:59,114 --> 00:04:00,364
- And what does that mean?

112
00:04:00,365 --> 00:04:02,783
- You owe me your life.

113
00:04:02,784 --> 00:04:04,451
I put you back together
good as new.

114
00:04:04,452 --> 00:04:06,161
You should be thanking me.

115
00:04:06,162 --> 00:04:07,287
- Thank you.

116
00:04:07,288 --> 00:04:10,416
Now, please,
get out of my head.

117
00:04:10,417 --> 00:04:14,086
- You might as well
get comfortable with me.

118
00:04:14,087 --> 00:04:15,921
'Cause I'm here to stay.

119
00:04:15,922 --> 00:04:18,090
<i>[uneasy music]</i>

120
00:04:18,091 --> 00:04:20,926
I chose this presentation

121
00:04:20,927 --> 00:04:26,056
because your recent memory
of this woman is... strong.

122
00:04:26,057 --> 00:04:30,394
<i>♪ ♪</i>

123
00:04:30,395 --> 00:04:32,104
So what's the problem?

124
00:04:32,105 --> 00:04:33,272
- The problem?

125
00:04:33,273 --> 00:04:36,150
The prob--Eva!

126
00:04:36,151 --> 00:04:39,153
- Why aren't you resting?
- Um...

127
00:04:39,154 --> 00:04:41,113
I think you need
to take me to med-bay.

128
00:04:41,114 --> 00:04:43,282
- What's wrong?

129
00:04:43,283 --> 00:04:46,785
- There is a woman
in my room, right--

130
00:04:46,786 --> 00:04:50,289
she's not there?

131
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:51,540
I ca--

132
00:04:51,541 --> 00:04:53,208
- Okay, okay, okay.

133
00:04:53,209 --> 00:04:54,710
Let's get you to Dr. Kabir.

134
00:04:54,711 --> 00:04:57,129
Okay, let's go.
- Nope!

135
00:04:57,130 --> 00:04:58,714
No, I'm fine.

136
00:04:58,715 --> 00:05:00,591
I'm good.
I don't need to go to med-bay.

137
00:05:00,592 --> 00:05:02,593
- Seems like you do.
- No, no, no, no.

138
00:05:02,594 --> 00:05:03,802
I'm fine. I'm all good.

139
00:05:03,803 --> 00:05:05,637
Yeah, just--I think I had
a bit of,

140
00:05:05,638 --> 00:05:06,805
like, a brain fog or something.

141
00:05:06,806 --> 00:05:08,432
But I'm--
I'm totally fine, yeah.

142
00:05:08,433 --> 00:05:09,725
Shouldn't you be on duty?

143
00:05:09,726 --> 00:05:11,268
- I took a break
to check on you.

144
00:05:11,269 --> 00:05:13,187
- Oh, thank you.
That is so nice.

145
00:05:13,188 --> 00:05:14,938
But I'm--I'm fine. I am good.

146
00:05:14,939 --> 00:05:16,106
No, I'm just gonna--

147
00:05:16,107 --> 00:05:17,608
just gonna sleep it off,
I think.

148
00:05:17,609 --> 00:05:18,734
Yeah, I'm just gonna rest up.
So you go.

149
00:05:18,735 --> 00:05:20,277
- Are you sure?
- I am sure.

150
00:05:20,278 --> 00:05:21,695
I promise. Mwah, mwah.

151
00:05:21,696 --> 00:05:23,781
Go. Go, go, go.
Go on, get out of here, you.

152
00:05:23,782 --> 00:05:25,157
[chuckles]
See you in a bit.

153
00:05:25,158 --> 00:05:27,368
All right, bye. Mwah. Bye.

154
00:05:27,369 --> 00:05:29,411
[gasps]

155
00:05:29,412 --> 00:05:30,704
You can frickin' control me?

156
00:05:30,705 --> 00:05:31,997
- [voice reverberates]
Don't try that again.

157
00:05:31,998 --> 00:05:33,040
- Answer me!

158
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:35,000
- I can't control you
completely.

159
00:05:35,001 --> 00:05:37,628
I can only protect myself.

160
00:05:37,629 --> 00:05:39,630
So far.

161
00:05:39,631 --> 00:05:41,131
- So far?

162
00:05:41,132 --> 00:05:44,051
- And it just occurred to me
why you're so resistant to me.

163
00:05:44,052 --> 00:05:47,346
I mean, you're already paired
with that female.

164
00:05:47,347 --> 00:05:49,473
- Paired with that female.
Yes, yes, yes.

165
00:05:49,474 --> 00:05:51,975
I am already paired
with that female,

166
00:05:51,976 --> 00:05:53,352
thank you very much.

167
00:05:53,353 --> 00:05:55,479
So this, right, whatever
this is, it's not gonna work.

168
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:58,315
So just get out of my head!

169
00:05:58,316 --> 00:06:00,401
- Now, come to bed.

170
00:06:00,402 --> 00:06:01,694
And get some rest.

171
00:06:01,695 --> 00:06:03,946
You're gonna need
your strength.

172
00:06:03,947 --> 00:06:05,280
- I'll take the chair.

173
00:06:05,281 --> 00:06:12,455
<i>♪ ♪</i>

174
00:06:14,457 --> 00:06:17,292
<i>[uplifting music]</i>

175
00:06:17,293 --> 00:06:24,467
<i>♪ ♪</i>

176
00:06:36,354 --> 00:06:38,147
- Dr. Kabir?

177
00:06:38,148 --> 00:06:40,983
I just checked in on James.

178
00:06:40,984 --> 00:06:42,401
I'm worried about him.

179
00:06:42,402 --> 00:06:43,902
- Oh.

180
00:06:43,903 --> 00:06:45,404
Tell me more.

181
00:06:45,405 --> 00:06:48,323
- Well, first he was
talking to the wall.

182
00:06:48,324 --> 00:06:49,783
- Talking to the wall?

183
00:06:49,784 --> 00:06:50,701
- I think.

184
00:06:50,702 --> 00:06:53,454
I mean, it was hard to tell.

185
00:06:53,455 --> 00:06:55,664
Then he couldn't talk at all.

186
00:06:55,665 --> 00:06:57,833
Like, he couldn't
get the words out.

187
00:06:57,834 --> 00:06:59,877
I'm just really worried
about him.

188
00:06:59,878 --> 00:07:01,545
- Okay.
Well, bring him to med-bay.

189
00:07:01,546 --> 00:07:03,505
- I tried.

190
00:07:03,506 --> 00:07:06,592
But he insisted that he's fine.

191
00:07:06,593 --> 00:07:09,178
He said he just has
a little brain fog,

192
00:07:09,179 --> 00:07:11,472
and he needs to rest.

193
00:07:11,473 --> 00:07:13,766
- I mean, he's probably right.

194
00:07:13,767 --> 00:07:16,018
You know,
he's been through hell.

195
00:07:16,019 --> 00:07:18,270
It will take time for him
to get back to normal.

196
00:07:18,271 --> 00:07:21,774
- It felt wrong leaving him
alone, but I'm on duty.

197
00:07:21,775 --> 00:07:24,818
- Okay, what was he doing
when you left him?

198
00:07:24,819 --> 00:07:27,154
- He said he was gonna sleep.

199
00:07:27,155 --> 00:07:28,238
- Good.

200
00:07:28,239 --> 00:07:29,990
That's the best thing for him.

201
00:07:29,991 --> 00:07:32,368
But I'll check in
on him later, okay?

202
00:07:32,369 --> 00:07:34,453
Don't worry.
- Thank you.

203
00:07:34,454 --> 00:07:37,414
<i>[tense music]</i>

204
00:07:37,415 --> 00:07:44,589
<i>♪ ♪</i>

205
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:51,929
- I've gotta tell you,
you almost dying down there,

206
00:07:51,930 --> 00:07:54,098
all I could think about was
how I was gonna lose you

207
00:07:54,099 --> 00:07:56,517
before we even got started.

208
00:07:56,518 --> 00:07:57,935
- Started?

209
00:07:57,936 --> 00:08:00,104
- Yeah, with this.

210
00:08:00,105 --> 00:08:01,147
Us.

211
00:08:03,149 --> 00:08:05,693
- I'm sorry, Ian.
- Oh, no, don't be sorry.

212
00:08:05,694 --> 00:08:07,194
I just wanted to make sure
that you knew

213
00:08:07,195 --> 00:08:09,989
how much I cared about you.

214
00:08:09,990 --> 00:08:11,365
- I do know.

215
00:08:11,366 --> 00:08:12,742
And I care about you too.

216
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:18,330
But if I'm gonna keep
the respect of the crew,

217
00:08:18,331 --> 00:08:20,958
I have to maintain
professional propriety.

218
00:08:20,959 --> 00:08:23,293
- Oh, come on.

219
00:08:23,294 --> 00:08:25,629
Surely you get to have a life
like everyone else.

220
00:08:25,630 --> 00:08:28,006
<i>[soft music]</i>

221
00:08:28,007 --> 00:08:33,470
- Sometimes I feel like
I'm split into two people.

222
00:08:33,471 --> 00:08:35,013
You know what I mean?

223
00:08:35,014 --> 00:08:39,768
Like, Captain Garnet
and Sharon Garnet.

224
00:08:39,769 --> 00:08:43,480
- Well, if I get a vote,
I vote for Sharon Garnet.

225
00:08:43,481 --> 00:08:46,400
[both chuckle]

226
00:08:46,401 --> 00:08:51,739
<i>♪ ♪</i>

227
00:08:51,740 --> 00:08:54,700
- Ian?
- Yeah?

228
00:08:54,701 --> 00:08:57,327
- We can't let anyone know
about this.

229
00:08:57,328 --> 00:08:58,872
- Know about what?

230
00:09:03,835 --> 00:09:04,918
- This.

231
00:09:04,919 --> 00:09:11,885
♪ ♪

232
00:09:14,679 --> 00:09:17,140
[breathing heavily]

233
00:09:20,435 --> 00:09:22,520
<i>- Captain Garnet
to the bridge.</i>

234
00:09:25,565 --> 00:09:27,483
- You have got
to be kidding me.

235
00:09:27,484 --> 00:09:31,071
- Looks like the universe cast
its vote for Captain Garnet.

236
00:09:34,449 --> 00:09:36,034
- [groans]

237
00:09:40,163 --> 00:09:41,622
- Report.

238
00:09:41,623 --> 00:09:43,457
- We got a garbled
distress beacon.

239
00:09:43,458 --> 00:09:44,875
It's on repeat,
but we can't make

240
00:09:44,876 --> 00:09:46,543
heads or tails of what it says.

241
00:09:46,544 --> 00:09:47,878
- Can you tell if it's GSA?

242
00:09:47,879 --> 00:09:49,797
- It was on
a GSA-encoded frequency.

243
00:09:49,798 --> 00:09:51,757
- Well, can you triangulate
its location?

244
00:09:51,758 --> 00:09:53,217
- Already did.

245
00:09:53,218 --> 00:09:54,927
Looks like there's
no solar system there.

246
00:09:54,928 --> 00:09:58,097
Just empty space, which is odd
but not unheard of.

247
00:09:58,098 --> 00:09:59,431
- All right.

248
00:09:59,432 --> 00:10:00,808
Set a course for the source
of the signal.

249
00:10:00,809 --> 00:10:02,686
Let's see if we can help.

250
00:10:20,662 --> 00:10:21,995
What the hell happened here?

251
00:10:21,996 --> 00:10:25,624
- The distress signal is
coming from the debris.

252
00:10:25,625 --> 00:10:26,917
It was an Ark.

253
00:10:26,918 --> 00:10:29,003
- Oh, God. Which one?

254
00:10:29,004 --> 00:10:31,171
- Can't tell.
I can't find any identifier.

255
00:10:31,172 --> 00:10:34,091
- Captain, we're being dragged
by a very strong gravity force.

256
00:10:34,092 --> 00:10:35,926
- Looks like we dropped
out of FTL

257
00:10:35,927 --> 00:10:37,594
right next to the gravity well.

258
00:10:37,595 --> 00:10:38,846
Not sure of its source,
but it seems

259
00:10:38,847 --> 00:10:40,305
like some form of dark matter.

260
00:10:40,306 --> 00:10:42,099
- Might I suggest
we hightail it out of here

261
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:44,435
before whatever did this
to the other Ark does it to us?

262
00:10:44,436 --> 00:10:45,477
- Agreed.

263
00:10:45,478 --> 00:10:47,312
Get us out of here, Kimi.

264
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:49,148
<i>[tense music]</i>

265
00:10:49,149 --> 00:10:51,650
[screeching]

266
00:10:51,651 --> 00:10:53,110
- That doesn't sound good.

267
00:10:53,111 --> 00:10:54,903
- Strong gravity waves
are bending space-time.

268
00:10:54,904 --> 00:10:57,364
It's interfering
with the FTL forming a bubble.

269
00:10:57,365 --> 00:10:59,825
- Kimi, try the NEPS engines.

270
00:10:59,826 --> 00:11:01,201
- The gravity pull
is too strong.

271
00:11:01,202 --> 00:11:03,120
- Damn it!
I can redirect resources

272
00:11:03,121 --> 00:11:05,205
from non-life support systems
to goose the NEPS.

273
00:11:05,206 --> 00:11:06,999
Maybe it'll be enough
to move us further out,

274
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,542
and then, I don't know,
hopefully the FTL will work.

275
00:11:08,543 --> 00:11:09,794
- Do it.

276
00:11:12,756 --> 00:11:16,800
[whirring]

277
00:11:16,801 --> 00:11:19,011
- NEPS overload.

278
00:11:19,012 --> 00:11:21,013
- There has to be
something else we can try.

279
00:11:21,014 --> 00:11:23,057
- I don't think there is.
- Kimi?

280
00:11:23,058 --> 00:11:24,850
- I got nothing.
- We're stranded here.

281
00:11:24,851 --> 00:11:27,269
- Eva, try redirecting power
from the FTL chargers.

282
00:11:27,270 --> 00:11:29,188
And, Kimi, send a K23
distress signal

283
00:11:29,189 --> 00:11:31,941
on the off chance someone is
around who can help us.

284
00:11:34,486 --> 00:11:35,569
[computer chirps]
- Sent.

285
00:11:35,570 --> 00:11:37,529
- Commencing now.

286
00:11:37,530 --> 00:11:39,323
[whirring]
- Look.

287
00:11:39,324 --> 00:11:41,284
The bloody debris disappeared.
How can that be?

288
00:11:44,871 --> 00:11:47,832
- Captain,
the systems are overloaded.

289
00:12:02,722 --> 00:12:05,641
<i>[eerie music]</i>

290
00:12:05,642 --> 00:12:10,771
<i>♪ ♪</i>

291
00:12:10,772 --> 00:12:12,147
- What the hell happened here?

292
00:12:12,148 --> 00:12:15,234
- The distress signal is
coming from the debris.

293
00:12:15,235 --> 00:12:16,485
It was an Ark.

294
00:12:16,486 --> 00:12:17,988
- Oh, God. Which one?

295
00:12:19,614 --> 00:12:22,366
[birds chirping]

296
00:12:22,367 --> 00:12:24,660
[computer chiming]

297
00:12:24,661 --> 00:12:26,453
- Let's see
if it's still playing

298
00:12:26,454 --> 00:12:28,664
the musical scale password
that worked last time.

299
00:12:28,665 --> 00:12:30,082
[beeping]

300
00:12:30,083 --> 00:12:33,836
[notes playing]

301
00:12:33,837 --> 00:12:39,258
♪ ♪

302
00:12:39,259 --> 00:12:40,802
Huh.

303
00:12:43,847 --> 00:12:46,807
[notes playing]

304
00:12:46,808 --> 00:12:53,773
♪ ♪

305
00:13:06,494 --> 00:13:08,329
- What's that?

306
00:13:08,330 --> 00:13:11,331
<i>[suspenseful music]</i>

307
00:13:11,332 --> 00:13:13,959
<i>♪ ♪</i>

308
00:13:13,960 --> 00:13:15,961
- That wasn't there
the last time we were here.

309
00:13:15,962 --> 00:13:17,963
I'd remember it.

310
00:13:17,964 --> 00:13:19,423
Wait!

311
00:13:19,424 --> 00:13:21,258
I don't think
you should touch it.

312
00:13:21,259 --> 00:13:28,433
<i>♪ ♪</i>

313
00:13:39,027 --> 00:13:40,611
- Looks like
the same style lettering

314
00:13:40,612 --> 00:13:42,530
as the slab we dug up.

315
00:13:51,831 --> 00:13:53,874
- This can't be.

316
00:13:53,875 --> 00:13:55,501
[door whirs]

317
00:13:55,502 --> 00:13:57,003
- Mr. Trust!

318
00:13:58,838 --> 00:14:01,298
We found this
at the underground entrance.

319
00:14:01,299 --> 00:14:04,510
- Well, the language
looks the same.

320
00:14:04,511 --> 00:14:05,803
Why didn't anyone see this

321
00:14:05,804 --> 00:14:06,929
the last time
you were out there?

322
00:14:06,930 --> 00:14:08,555
- Alicia says
it wasn't there then.

323
00:14:08,556 --> 00:14:10,474
- So someone put it there
for you to find.

324
00:14:10,475 --> 00:14:12,643
Interesting.

325
00:14:12,644 --> 00:14:14,561
And tell me, the lettering,
has it been the same

326
00:14:14,562 --> 00:14:16,313
the whole time it's been
in your possession?

327
00:14:16,314 --> 00:14:17,523
- Why wouldn't it be?

328
00:14:17,524 --> 00:14:19,316
- Because the symbols
on the slab aren't.

329
00:14:19,317 --> 00:14:21,068
- What?

330
00:14:21,069 --> 00:14:22,361
- Look.

331
00:14:22,362 --> 00:14:24,656
I took these reference photos
when it first came in.

332
00:14:30,036 --> 00:14:31,370
- It changed.

333
00:14:31,371 --> 00:14:33,748
[keypad beeps]

334
00:14:36,668 --> 00:14:39,920
How is that possible?

335
00:14:39,921 --> 00:14:44,383
These words are
chiseled in stone.

336
00:14:44,384 --> 00:14:47,344
<i>[tense music]</i>

337
00:14:47,345 --> 00:14:54,519
<i>♪ ♪</i>

338
00:14:55,478 --> 00:14:58,023
[both grunting]

339
00:15:07,032 --> 00:15:08,490
- [groans]

340
00:15:08,491 --> 00:15:11,619
[both grunting]

341
00:15:15,165 --> 00:15:16,540
- Who sent you?
[groans]

342
00:15:16,541 --> 00:15:23,422
<i>♪ ♪</i>

343
00:15:23,423 --> 00:15:27,134
<i>- 24, report.
Sending backup.</i>

344
00:15:27,135 --> 00:15:34,059
<i>♪ ♪</i>

345
00:15:36,269 --> 00:15:37,978
[door beeps]

346
00:15:37,979 --> 00:15:39,355
- I'm not dressed.

347
00:15:39,356 --> 00:15:40,856
This better be an emergency.

348
00:15:40,857 --> 00:15:43,484
- It is.
- Oh, it's you.

349
00:15:43,485 --> 00:15:44,736
Come in.

350
00:15:48,907 --> 00:15:50,657
You have something to report?

351
00:15:50,658 --> 00:15:52,409
- Strickland escaped.

352
00:15:52,410 --> 00:15:54,286
- What? How?

353
00:15:54,287 --> 00:15:55,662
- I don't know.

354
00:15:55,663 --> 00:15:58,123
I found Jeffries on the floor,
locked in the cell.

355
00:15:58,124 --> 00:15:59,208
- Idiot!

356
00:15:59,209 --> 00:16:01,126
I should've done it myself.

357
00:16:01,127 --> 00:16:02,503
Is Jeffries even alive?

358
00:16:02,504 --> 00:16:04,380
- Yes, ma'am.

359
00:16:04,381 --> 00:16:08,217
- Tell him
to get his ass out there

360
00:16:08,218 --> 00:16:13,764
with every woman and man
we have and find Felix.

361
00:16:13,765 --> 00:16:15,599
That includes you.

362
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:17,518
Go. I'll catch up.

363
00:16:17,519 --> 00:16:23,190
<i>♪ ♪</i>

364
00:16:23,191 --> 00:16:25,818
- Alicia.

365
00:16:25,819 --> 00:16:28,195
- Angus, it's so late.

366
00:16:28,196 --> 00:16:29,655
- Alicia.

367
00:16:29,656 --> 00:16:31,281
- [gasps] Felix?

368
00:16:31,282 --> 00:16:32,992
- Sorry, I didn't mean
to startle you.

369
00:16:32,993 --> 00:16:34,868
I couldn't be seen
in the corridor knocking,

370
00:16:34,869 --> 00:16:36,286
so I used my code to enter.

371
00:16:36,287 --> 00:16:38,372
I hope you don't mind.

372
00:16:38,373 --> 00:16:40,040
- What's going on?

373
00:16:40,041 --> 00:16:43,168
- They're going to say
I attacked a guard and escaped,

374
00:16:43,169 --> 00:16:45,504
but the truth is,
he was trying to kill me.

375
00:16:45,505 --> 00:16:47,089
- Oh, my God, are you--

376
00:16:47,090 --> 00:16:48,882
- I've got to go into hiding
before they try it again.

377
00:16:48,883 --> 00:16:51,051
- Wait. Who's "they"?

378
00:16:51,052 --> 00:16:52,678
- That's what I need you
to find out.

379
00:16:52,679 --> 00:16:54,138
He was wearing a mask.

380
00:16:54,139 --> 00:16:58,642
My guess is, Ves is behind
all this, but I have no proof.

381
00:16:58,643 --> 00:17:00,185
- Okay, so what do you
want me to do?

382
00:17:00,186 --> 00:17:02,271
- I need you to hack
into the database logs

383
00:17:02,272 --> 00:17:04,398
that Hammond was looking at
regarding Ves.

384
00:17:04,399 --> 00:17:05,816
See what she's hiding.

385
00:17:05,817 --> 00:17:07,818
- But that was all redacted
beyond my clearance.

386
00:17:07,819 --> 00:17:09,278
That encryption codec
is beyond--

387
00:17:09,279 --> 00:17:13,533
- Alicia, I need that
information to clear my name.

388
00:17:16,494 --> 00:17:18,163
- Okay.

389
00:17:21,249 --> 00:17:24,043
If Hammond was careless enough
not to wipe his tracks,

390
00:17:24,044 --> 00:17:25,919
I could probably go back
in the binary code

391
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:29,590
and find out what password
he entered to get in.

392
00:17:29,591 --> 00:17:30,966
- Never doubted
you could do it.

393
00:17:30,967 --> 00:17:34,011
I'll be hiding out
in the forest.

394
00:17:34,012 --> 00:17:35,220
- Wait.

395
00:17:35,221 --> 00:17:38,015
[device beeping]

396
00:17:38,016 --> 00:17:40,517
I've turned off the tracking
and set an encrypted channel

397
00:17:40,518 --> 00:17:42,227
I can reach you on.

398
00:17:42,228 --> 00:17:43,687
- Thank you.

399
00:17:43,688 --> 00:17:45,731
If you find anything,
take it to Zed Avega.

400
00:17:45,732 --> 00:17:47,024
I've come to trust him.

401
00:17:47,025 --> 00:17:48,692
But don't trust anyone else.

402
00:17:48,693 --> 00:17:50,611
We don't know
who's working with Ves.

403
00:17:50,612 --> 00:17:57,744
<i>♪ ♪</i>

404
00:18:02,540 --> 00:18:04,209
- [sighs]

405
00:18:11,007 --> 00:18:14,635
<i>[tense music]</i>

406
00:18:14,636 --> 00:18:16,553
- What the hell happened here?

407
00:18:16,554 --> 00:18:19,264
- The distress signal is
coming from the debris.

408
00:18:19,265 --> 00:18:20,224
It was an Ark.

409
00:18:20,225 --> 00:18:21,558
- Okay.

410
00:18:21,559 --> 00:18:24,979
And what else do you think
we should try?

411
00:18:24,980 --> 00:18:28,273
But there has to be a way.

412
00:18:28,274 --> 00:18:29,483
- There's two of you now?

413
00:18:29,484 --> 00:18:31,819
- 219.
- Huh?

414
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:35,906
- There are
219 instances of me.

415
00:18:35,907 --> 00:18:38,867
<i>[unsettling music]</i>

416
00:18:38,868 --> 00:18:44,707
<i>♪ ♪</i>

417
00:18:44,708 --> 00:18:46,333
- Okay.
[clears throat]

418
00:18:46,334 --> 00:18:48,544
What the hell is going on?
- All right.

419
00:18:48,545 --> 00:18:52,172
I've told you this 219 times,
but I'll try it again.

420
00:18:52,173 --> 00:18:55,092
We're trapped in a closed
causal temporal recursion.

421
00:18:55,093 --> 00:18:56,260
- A what now?

422
00:18:56,261 --> 00:18:57,886
- A time loop.
- Oh, right.

423
00:18:57,887 --> 00:18:59,555
A time loop. Okay.

424
00:18:59,556 --> 00:19:01,348
- It's being caused
by the warping of space-time

425
00:19:01,349 --> 00:19:03,308
by a gravity well
holding us here.

426
00:19:03,309 --> 00:19:04,685
- Not buying it.

427
00:19:04,686 --> 00:19:06,145
- Of course not. You never do.

428
00:19:06,146 --> 00:19:07,980
- Yeah, because you're on
a chip in my head, right?

429
00:19:07,981 --> 00:19:09,648
So you see what I see.

430
00:19:09,649 --> 00:19:11,233
So how would you know about
this supposed time loop,

431
00:19:11,234 --> 00:19:12,317
but I don't?

432
00:19:12,318 --> 00:19:13,736
- Because I'm not
flesh and blood.

433
00:19:13,737 --> 00:19:15,362
I'm software in a quantum chip.

434
00:19:15,363 --> 00:19:16,530
I don't perceive time
as you do.

435
00:19:16,531 --> 00:19:18,615
- Mm. Bullshit.

436
00:19:18,616 --> 00:19:22,369
- Look, they've done this
219 times.

437
00:19:22,370 --> 00:19:23,579
I keep trying,
but I can't figure out

438
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:24,788
how to get us out of it.

439
00:19:24,789 --> 00:19:26,332
- Uh-huh.
- You have to trust me

440
00:19:26,333 --> 00:19:27,541
if we're going to have
a chance.

441
00:19:27,542 --> 00:19:30,252
Otherwise, we blow up
over and over again.

442
00:19:30,253 --> 00:19:31,962
- And why do you care?

443
00:19:31,963 --> 00:19:34,631
Seriously, why do you care
if we get out of this or not?

444
00:19:34,632 --> 00:19:36,258
- You're still not getting it.

445
00:19:36,259 --> 00:19:39,094
You and I have to get to
Homebase as soon as possible.

446
00:19:39,095 --> 00:19:41,847
To do that, we have to get
out of this loop.

447
00:19:41,848 --> 00:19:45,893
And to do that,
I really need your cooperation.

448
00:19:45,894 --> 00:19:48,103
- [breathes deeply]

449
00:19:48,104 --> 00:19:50,147
Okay.

450
00:19:50,148 --> 00:19:51,565
I'll bite.

451
00:19:51,566 --> 00:19:52,900
What is it that you would
want me to do?

452
00:19:52,901 --> 00:19:54,610
- Okay.
In past instances,

453
00:19:54,611 --> 00:19:56,820
I've been able to convince you
to look at engineering,

454
00:19:56,821 --> 00:19:58,530
sub-engineering,
and electrical.

455
00:19:58,531 --> 00:19:59,656
But no matter what I try,

456
00:19:59,657 --> 00:20:01,241
I can't get you
to go to the bridge.

457
00:20:01,242 --> 00:20:02,826
- Yeah, you're damn straight.

458
00:20:02,827 --> 00:20:04,787
Because I can't trust you
not to try and make me

459
00:20:04,788 --> 00:20:06,080
do something
that's gonna endanger

460
00:20:06,081 --> 00:20:07,539
this entire ship and its crew.

461
00:20:07,540 --> 00:20:08,957
- Which is what you tell me
every time.

462
00:20:08,958 --> 00:20:11,669
But I need to go to the bridge
to see exactly what happens.

463
00:20:11,670 --> 00:20:14,546
- No, not a chance.

464
00:20:14,547 --> 00:20:17,174
- And in every instance,
I can't understand

465
00:20:17,175 --> 00:20:19,927
why you won't go and save Eva.

466
00:20:19,928 --> 00:20:21,929
She's critically injured
on the bridge.

467
00:20:21,930 --> 00:20:24,848
<i>[tense music]</i>

468
00:20:24,849 --> 00:20:27,226
<i>♪ ♪</i>

469
00:20:27,227 --> 00:20:28,811
- Oh! Sorry.

470
00:20:28,812 --> 00:20:30,354
Dr. Kabir.
- Yeah.

471
00:20:30,355 --> 00:20:32,439
- Uh, listen, is, uh--
is Eva okay?

472
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:34,400
- Oh.
Far as I know, she's fine.

473
00:20:34,401 --> 00:20:36,443
But how about you?
Are you okay?

474
00:20:36,444 --> 00:20:38,529
- Yeah, yeah.
No, I'm, uh--I'm fine.

475
00:20:38,530 --> 00:20:43,784
<i>♪ ♪</i>

476
00:20:43,785 --> 00:20:45,160
- I can redirect resources

477
00:20:45,161 --> 00:20:46,787
from non-life support systems
to goose the NEPS.

478
00:20:46,788 --> 00:20:48,038
Maybe that'll be enough
to move us further out.

479
00:20:48,039 --> 00:20:49,915
And then hopefully
the FTL will work.

480
00:20:49,916 --> 00:20:50,917
- Do it.

481
00:20:54,337 --> 00:20:55,921
- Oh, look. You lied.

482
00:20:55,922 --> 00:20:57,297
- [shushing]

483
00:20:57,298 --> 00:20:58,758
I need to watch.

484
00:21:01,136 --> 00:21:04,054
[whirring]

485
00:21:04,055 --> 00:21:05,597
- NEPS overload.

486
00:21:05,598 --> 00:21:06,640
- There has to be
something else we can try.

487
00:21:06,641 --> 00:21:08,308
- I don't think there is.

488
00:21:08,309 --> 00:21:09,852
- Uh, Kimi?

489
00:21:09,853 --> 00:21:12,396
- I got nothing. I'm sorry.

490
00:21:12,397 --> 00:21:14,064
- Well, I think
we're stranded here.

491
00:21:14,065 --> 00:21:16,191
- Eva, try redirecting power
from the FTL chargers.

492
00:21:16,192 --> 00:21:18,068
And, Kimi, send a K23
distress signal

493
00:21:18,069 --> 00:21:20,904
on the off chance someone
is around who can help us.

494
00:21:20,905 --> 00:21:23,115
[computer chirps]
- Sent.

495
00:21:23,116 --> 00:21:24,533
- Commencing now.

496
00:21:24,534 --> 00:21:25,868
- Look.

497
00:21:25,869 --> 00:21:28,120
The bloody debris disappeared.
How can that be?

498
00:21:28,121 --> 00:21:29,705
[computer chirps]

499
00:21:29,706 --> 00:21:32,959
- Captain, the systems
are overloaded.

500
00:21:47,182 --> 00:21:50,100
<i>[tense music]</i>

501
00:21:50,101 --> 00:21:52,728
<i>♪ ♪</i>

502
00:21:52,729 --> 00:21:55,731
- But there has to be a way.

503
00:21:55,732 --> 00:21:57,900
- Wait, there's two of you now?

504
00:21:57,901 --> 00:22:00,194
- 220.

505
00:22:00,195 --> 00:22:01,488
- Huh?

506
00:22:03,698 --> 00:22:08,078
- There are
220 instances of me.

507
00:22:11,748 --> 00:22:13,665
- The guard was
bringing Strickland dinner.

508
00:22:13,666 --> 00:22:15,918
Felix jumped him
and broke his arm.

509
00:22:15,919 --> 00:22:17,628
- Do we have any idea why?

510
00:22:17,629 --> 00:22:19,129
- What do you mean?

511
00:22:19,130 --> 00:22:22,132
Felix Strickland murdered
Hammond in cold blood

512
00:22:22,133 --> 00:22:24,885
and wanted to escape without
facing the consequences.

513
00:22:24,886 --> 00:22:28,222
- That doesn't sound like the
Strickland I've come to know.

514
00:22:28,223 --> 00:22:30,474
- You know a different man
than I do.

515
00:22:30,475 --> 00:22:35,229
As far as I'm concerned,
his escape proves his guilt.

516
00:22:35,230 --> 00:22:37,439
We need to hunt him down.

517
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:40,442
Alive or dead, if necessary.

518
00:22:40,443 --> 00:22:42,277
- Slow down, Ves.

519
00:22:42,278 --> 00:22:43,487
We don't even have a motive

520
00:22:43,488 --> 00:22:46,198
for why Felix would
kill Hammond.

521
00:22:46,199 --> 00:22:49,076
I want to talk to the guard.
- You do that.

522
00:22:49,077 --> 00:22:51,871
I'm sure he'll tell you
the same thing he told me.

523
00:22:54,833 --> 00:22:56,876
- [sighs]

524
00:22:58,628 --> 00:23:01,296
- Mr. Avega.

525
00:23:01,297 --> 00:23:02,798
I have some information.

526
00:23:02,799 --> 00:23:04,550
And Felix Strickland said
you're the only one

527
00:23:04,551 --> 00:23:06,009
I should trust it with.

528
00:23:06,010 --> 00:23:08,470
- Okay.

529
00:23:08,471 --> 00:23:10,180
- I hacked
into the GSA database

530
00:23:10,181 --> 00:23:12,182
to find out
what Hammond was investigating.

531
00:23:12,183 --> 00:23:13,851
That's what I found.

532
00:23:13,852 --> 00:23:16,937
- Ves was a commander
of a Black Thorn unit.

533
00:23:16,938 --> 00:23:18,230
- Yes.

534
00:23:18,231 --> 00:23:20,983
The deepest secret black ops
division of the GSA.

535
00:23:20,984 --> 00:23:22,526
I never believed
it even existed.

536
00:23:22,527 --> 00:23:24,236
- Oh, it exists.

537
00:23:24,237 --> 00:23:27,948
I've had firsthand experience
with that nasty little group.

538
00:23:27,949 --> 00:23:29,950
I killed three of them myself
when they were sent

539
00:23:29,951 --> 00:23:32,619
to assassinate me
back on Earth.

540
00:23:32,620 --> 00:23:34,079
- They tried
to assassinate you?

541
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:38,208
- I was a very high-ranking
EF commander.

542
00:23:38,209 --> 00:23:40,544
- I wonder if that means
the EF hunters

543
00:23:40,545 --> 00:23:42,379
are also part of Black Thorn.

544
00:23:42,380 --> 00:23:44,214
- Why would that be relevant?

545
00:23:44,215 --> 00:23:46,175
- Because we have one
in our jail.

546
00:23:46,176 --> 00:23:49,178
<i>[tense music]</i>

547
00:23:49,179 --> 00:23:53,515
- I can't confirm or deny
the existence of Black Thorn.

548
00:23:53,516 --> 00:23:55,185
- [chuckles]

549
00:23:56,728 --> 00:24:01,815
I know you were an EF hunter.

550
00:24:01,816 --> 00:24:04,693
You've admitted that.

551
00:24:04,694 --> 00:24:06,445
- Let me guess.

552
00:24:06,446 --> 00:24:11,283
Given the two of you were EF,
you don't like me very much?

553
00:24:11,284 --> 00:24:13,118
- Not at all, in fact.

554
00:24:13,119 --> 00:24:14,870
- Oh.

555
00:24:14,871 --> 00:24:16,455
What you gonna do, kill me?

556
00:24:16,456 --> 00:24:17,664
- Hmm.

557
00:24:17,665 --> 00:24:20,167
[breathes deeply]

558
00:24:20,168 --> 00:24:22,045
[chuckles]

559
00:24:26,341 --> 00:24:28,258
Let's try this.

560
00:24:28,259 --> 00:24:32,763
Hypothetically,
if Black Thorn existed,

561
00:24:32,764 --> 00:24:37,227
would EF hunters be part
of that organization?

562
00:24:45,944 --> 00:24:49,990
- I can't confirm or deny
the existence of Black Thorn.

563
00:24:52,283 --> 00:24:55,285
- Wasting my time.

564
00:24:55,286 --> 00:24:58,664
- People who know too much
can get killed in this jail.

565
00:24:58,665 --> 00:25:01,625
<i>[tense music]</i>

566
00:25:01,626 --> 00:25:04,211
<i>♪ ♪</i>

567
00:25:04,212 --> 00:25:09,091
- But if I were able
to get you released,

568
00:25:09,092 --> 00:25:12,178
I'm guessing
suddenly you'd know a lot.

569
00:25:14,848 --> 00:25:16,724
[knock at door]

570
00:25:16,725 --> 00:25:20,018
It's open.

571
00:25:20,019 --> 00:25:22,604
- You wanted to see me?

572
00:25:22,605 --> 00:25:25,274
- Yes.

573
00:25:25,275 --> 00:25:28,610
You're the only other person
who knows about the situation.

574
00:25:28,611 --> 00:25:31,113
And the irony is,
I can't consult

575
00:25:31,114 --> 00:25:33,282
either one of my
co-council members,

576
00:25:33,283 --> 00:25:34,575
Strickland or Ves.

577
00:25:34,576 --> 00:25:36,535
- What did Lati say?

578
00:25:36,536 --> 00:25:39,038
- Not much.

579
00:25:39,039 --> 00:25:42,666
She wants to be released
before she'll talk.

580
00:25:42,667 --> 00:25:44,127
- Are you gonna let her go?

581
00:25:46,546 --> 00:25:48,172
- I don't know.

582
00:25:48,173 --> 00:25:52,051
It's a bit like deciding
between drinking arsenic

583
00:25:52,052 --> 00:25:55,679
and drinking strychnine.

584
00:25:55,680 --> 00:25:56,972
Either one will kill you.

585
00:25:56,973 --> 00:25:58,766
- I guess it depends
on who's arsenic

586
00:25:58,767 --> 00:26:00,851
and who's strychnine
in this metaphor.

587
00:26:00,852 --> 00:26:01,977
- What do you mean?

588
00:26:01,978 --> 00:26:04,813
- To me, Ves is arsenic.

589
00:26:04,814 --> 00:26:06,440
You know, it's tasteless,
it's odorless,

590
00:26:06,441 --> 00:26:09,443
and very difficult
to detect in an autopsy.

591
00:26:09,444 --> 00:26:11,028
She could get away
with killing the admiral,

592
00:26:11,029 --> 00:26:13,906
but you may never know
what else she's hiding

593
00:26:13,907 --> 00:26:15,491
if you don't make a deal
with Lati.

594
00:26:15,492 --> 00:26:16,992
- Uh-huh.

595
00:26:16,993 --> 00:26:20,287
- Strychnine is violent
and obvious, like Lati.

596
00:26:20,288 --> 00:26:21,789
- [chuckles]

597
00:26:21,790 --> 00:26:23,791
- I really tortured
that metaphor, didn't I?

598
00:26:23,792 --> 00:26:26,585
- Oh, yes, you did.

599
00:26:26,586 --> 00:26:28,212
- Sorry.

600
00:26:28,213 --> 00:26:33,467
- But in your unique way,
Alicia, you made a valid point.

601
00:26:33,468 --> 00:26:34,718
Lati is an open book,

602
00:26:34,719 --> 00:26:36,428
and we know exactly
what she's done.

603
00:26:36,429 --> 00:26:38,389
- Or attempted to do.

604
00:26:38,390 --> 00:26:42,059
She didn't actually succeed
in killing Eva or me.

605
00:26:42,060 --> 00:26:44,561
- But she was an EF hunter.

606
00:26:44,562 --> 00:26:46,689
Who knows how many of my
friends and colleagues

607
00:26:46,690 --> 00:26:48,273
she killed.

608
00:26:48,274 --> 00:26:53,947
EF hunters were the worst
of the worst in my world.

609
00:26:58,159 --> 00:27:00,161
They killed many of my friends.

610
00:27:02,914 --> 00:27:06,041
- That may be true,
but we were at war.

611
00:27:06,042 --> 00:27:09,128
I mean, the EF killed
a lot of my friends.

612
00:27:09,129 --> 00:27:12,339
Should I hate you for every
GSA person you killed?

613
00:27:12,340 --> 00:27:14,425
- Huh.

614
00:27:14,426 --> 00:27:18,345
Alicia, you're very wise
for such a young age.

615
00:27:18,346 --> 00:27:19,806
- Thank you.

616
00:27:28,690 --> 00:27:30,025
- What are you doing?

617
00:27:36,072 --> 00:27:37,614
- Lati didn't kill any EF.

618
00:27:37,615 --> 00:27:39,325
- How do you know that?

619
00:27:39,326 --> 00:27:42,453
- Oh, I hacked the admiral's
password, remember?

620
00:27:42,454 --> 00:27:46,623
I used it to remove all
the redactions on her record.

621
00:27:46,624 --> 00:27:49,835
Lati was on "Ark 15"
her whole tour of duty.

622
00:27:49,836 --> 00:27:51,462
There were no EF onboard.

623
00:27:51,463 --> 00:27:55,425
- Still, she was and is
an attempted murderer.

624
00:27:57,052 --> 00:27:58,552
- I wonder
what Ves is trying to hide

625
00:27:58,553 --> 00:28:01,513
so badly that she would
murder the admiral

626
00:28:01,514 --> 00:28:03,599
and frame Felix for it.

627
00:28:03,600 --> 00:28:05,476
- I can only imagine.

628
00:28:05,477 --> 00:28:08,104
- So are you gonna make a deal
with Lati and find out?

629
00:28:11,566 --> 00:28:13,692
- I can't find
any sort of mechanism

630
00:28:13,693 --> 00:28:15,277
to change this lettering.

631
00:28:15,278 --> 00:28:19,698
It's seemingly chiseled
indelibly into the damn stone.

632
00:28:19,699 --> 00:28:22,660
- Do you think
the triangle we brought back

633
00:28:22,661 --> 00:28:23,702
is made of the same material?

634
00:28:23,703 --> 00:28:24,828
- I don't know.

635
00:28:24,829 --> 00:28:26,122
Bring it over here.

636
00:28:36,591 --> 00:28:38,050
- Oh.

637
00:28:38,051 --> 00:28:39,843
- Fascinating.

638
00:28:39,844 --> 00:28:43,472
<i>[suspenseful music]</i>

639
00:28:43,473 --> 00:28:44,848
[sighs]

640
00:28:44,849 --> 00:28:51,438
<i>♪ ♪</i>

641
00:28:51,439 --> 00:28:52,690
- Wait.
- [yelps]

642
00:28:52,691 --> 00:28:55,192
Boy, I'm on 22 hours
in this lab with no sleep.

643
00:28:55,193 --> 00:28:56,735
You nearly gave me
a heart attack.

644
00:28:56,736 --> 00:28:59,071
- Sorry.

645
00:28:59,072 --> 00:29:01,824
Do you think it's a good idea
to put that in the hole?

646
00:29:01,825 --> 00:29:03,450
Like, it could explode
or something.

647
00:29:03,451 --> 00:29:05,829
- There's only one way
to find out.

648
00:29:09,666 --> 00:29:11,084
- [exclaims]

649
00:29:15,714 --> 00:29:17,673
Wow.

650
00:29:17,674 --> 00:29:20,134
- [sighs]

651
00:29:20,135 --> 00:29:23,178
Well, that was anticlimactic.

652
00:29:23,179 --> 00:29:24,597
- I thought it was cool.

653
00:29:28,727 --> 00:29:30,894
- Wait, this thing has
started emitting sound waves

654
00:29:30,895 --> 00:29:32,313
outside of our hearing range.

655
00:29:32,314 --> 00:29:34,648
- It wasn't doing that
before, right?

656
00:29:34,649 --> 00:29:36,066
- Nope.

657
00:29:36,067 --> 00:29:37,860
Let me try and use
Alicia's technique

658
00:29:37,861 --> 00:29:40,696
to drop the frequency.

659
00:29:40,697 --> 00:29:42,574
Right, can you hear anything?

660
00:29:43,825 --> 00:29:46,201
- No.

661
00:29:46,202 --> 00:29:48,370
- That should've worked.

662
00:29:48,371 --> 00:29:50,622
So the sounds can be detected
by our instrumentation,

663
00:29:50,623 --> 00:29:52,750
but they can't be adjusted
so that we can hear them.

664
00:29:52,751 --> 00:29:54,793
- [groans]

665
00:29:54,794 --> 00:29:57,254
Uh...

666
00:29:57,255 --> 00:29:59,256
so electronics can detect it?

667
00:29:59,257 --> 00:30:01,217
- Mm-hmm.

668
00:30:02,510 --> 00:30:04,261
- Kelly could hear sounds
in the underground

669
00:30:04,262 --> 00:30:06,847
that I couldn't hear when
she had her implants turned on.

670
00:30:06,848 --> 00:30:08,849
Maybe she'll be able
to hear it.

671
00:30:08,850 --> 00:30:10,434
- So what are you waiting for?

672
00:30:10,435 --> 00:30:13,604
Bring her over here.
- I'll bring her over here.

673
00:30:13,605 --> 00:30:17,651
<i>♪ ♪</i>

674
00:30:20,028 --> 00:30:21,570
- [scoffs]

675
00:30:21,571 --> 00:30:24,740
You asked to see me?

676
00:30:24,741 --> 00:30:26,241
- Yeah.

677
00:30:26,242 --> 00:30:28,077
Just thought you should know,

678
00:30:28,078 --> 00:30:30,662
Avega wants me
to roll over on you.

679
00:30:30,663 --> 00:30:33,083
- I'm not sure
what you're talking about.

680
00:30:36,378 --> 00:30:39,797
- Don't worry.
I kept my mouth shut.

681
00:30:39,798 --> 00:30:42,800
Once a Thorn, always a Thorn.

682
00:30:42,801 --> 00:30:45,344
- That's adorable.

683
00:30:45,345 --> 00:30:47,304
Just have no idea
what that means.

684
00:30:47,305 --> 00:30:48,764
- Good.

685
00:30:48,765 --> 00:30:51,683
Then I guess I can tell them
whatever they want to know.

686
00:30:51,684 --> 00:30:54,311
- And what exactly
do they want to know?

687
00:30:54,312 --> 00:30:56,689
- They think you killed
that admiral.

688
00:30:56,690 --> 00:30:58,692
- I don't know
what you're talking about.

689
00:31:00,694 --> 00:31:04,029
- They also think
that you framed Strickland

690
00:31:04,030 --> 00:31:07,533
and then sent someone
to kill him in jail.

691
00:31:07,534 --> 00:31:10,620
- Well, they have
quite the imagination.

692
00:31:15,083 --> 00:31:17,793
- You know I was here, right?

693
00:31:17,794 --> 00:31:21,130
When that Thorn came in
to kill Strickland?

694
00:31:21,131 --> 00:31:22,464
And I can ID the guy.

695
00:31:22,465 --> 00:31:26,176
- What does that
have to do with me?

696
00:31:26,177 --> 00:31:29,304
- Well, he kind of said
a few things

697
00:31:29,305 --> 00:31:32,182
that incriminate you.

698
00:31:32,183 --> 00:31:34,685
- And I assume
you want something.

699
00:31:34,686 --> 00:31:36,020
- Only my freedom.

700
00:31:36,021 --> 00:31:39,189
- [chuckles]
Well, that's impossible.

701
00:31:39,190 --> 00:31:40,774
You're an attempted murderer.

702
00:31:40,775 --> 00:31:43,235
- And you're a successful one.

703
00:31:43,236 --> 00:31:47,240
- If that were true,
why wouldn't I just kill you?

704
00:31:49,743 --> 00:31:52,828
<i>[ominous music]</i>

705
00:31:52,829 --> 00:31:54,538
- Go for it.

706
00:31:54,539 --> 00:31:57,041
<i>- It's like you want me to.</i>

707
00:31:57,042 --> 00:31:58,709
<i>- No.</i>

708
00:31:58,710 --> 00:32:01,712
<i>I actually don't believe
you would kill a fellow Thorn.</i>

709
00:32:01,713 --> 00:32:04,006
<i>- You see,
that's where you're wrong.</i>

710
00:32:04,007 --> 00:32:06,342
If I could kill an admiral,

711
00:32:06,343 --> 00:32:09,928
why would I hesitate
for one second

712
00:32:09,929 --> 00:32:12,848
to kill a nobody like you?

713
00:32:12,849 --> 00:32:13,932
- And I thought we had

714
00:32:13,933 --> 00:32:16,436
that Black Thorn
sisterhood thing going.

715
00:32:18,188 --> 00:32:19,938
- Step aside, Zed.

716
00:32:19,939 --> 00:32:22,358
[grunts, gasps]

717
00:32:22,359 --> 00:32:25,027
- You forgot I have
the same training you do.

718
00:32:25,028 --> 00:32:26,904
- You don't have
the authority to do this.

719
00:32:26,905 --> 00:32:28,447
- Oh, I think I do.

720
00:32:28,448 --> 00:32:31,408
- Just so you know, I didn't
see or hear the attacker,

721
00:32:31,409 --> 00:32:33,202
much less know
if he was Black Thorn.

722
00:32:33,203 --> 00:32:35,621
- I won't forget this.

723
00:32:35,622 --> 00:32:36,706
- Neither will I.

724
00:32:38,917 --> 00:32:40,167
[door slams]

725
00:32:40,168 --> 00:32:41,628
It was kind of fun.

726
00:32:46,049 --> 00:32:48,759
<i>[suspenseful music]</i>

727
00:32:48,760 --> 00:32:50,886
- Don't worry, Ves.

728
00:32:50,887 --> 00:32:52,596
You'll get a fair trial.

729
00:32:52,597 --> 00:32:59,562
<i>♪ ♪</i>

730
00:33:06,861 --> 00:33:09,322
[beeping]

731
00:33:11,032 --> 00:33:13,367
- All right, I've told you this
about 230 times,

732
00:33:13,368 --> 00:33:14,618
but I'll try again.

733
00:33:14,619 --> 00:33:16,120
We're trapped in a time loop.

734
00:33:16,121 --> 00:33:18,330
- Ooh. Time loop.
I'm not buying it.

735
00:33:18,331 --> 00:33:19,456
- Of course not.

736
00:33:19,457 --> 00:33:21,083
All right,
I'm just gonna jump ahead.

737
00:33:21,084 --> 00:33:24,294
Eva is critically injured
on the bridge.

738
00:33:24,295 --> 00:33:25,587
- What?

739
00:33:25,588 --> 00:33:27,589
- Dr. Kabir is about
to come around the corner.

740
00:33:27,590 --> 00:33:29,091
- What?

741
00:33:29,092 --> 00:33:31,093
- Oh!
both: Sorry.

742
00:33:31,094 --> 00:33:34,388
Dr. Kabir, listen, is Eva okay?

743
00:33:34,389 --> 00:33:36,390
both: As far as I know,
she's fine.

744
00:33:36,391 --> 00:33:38,350
But how about you?

745
00:33:38,351 --> 00:33:40,018
Are you okay?

746
00:33:40,019 --> 00:33:42,147
both: Yeah, I'm fine.

747
00:33:47,360 --> 00:33:48,610
- How did you know
what we were gonna say?

748
00:33:48,611 --> 00:33:50,904
- I keep telling you,
we're in a time loop.

749
00:33:50,905 --> 00:33:53,198
I've heard it
hundreds of times.

750
00:33:53,199 --> 00:33:55,993
- Do it.

751
00:33:55,994 --> 00:33:57,536
- You lied.
- Shh.

752
00:33:57,537 --> 00:34:00,205
I need to watch.

753
00:34:00,206 --> 00:34:01,915
- NEPS overload.

754
00:34:01,916 --> 00:34:03,500
- There has to be
something else we can try.

755
00:34:03,501 --> 00:34:05,586
- I don't think there is.

756
00:34:05,587 --> 00:34:06,837
- Kimi?
- I got nothing.

757
00:34:06,838 --> 00:34:07,880
I'm sorry.
- Great.

758
00:34:07,881 --> 00:34:09,423
I think we're stranded.

759
00:34:09,424 --> 00:34:11,925
- Eva, try redirecting power
from the FTL chargers.

760
00:34:11,926 --> 00:34:13,135
- Stop her
from redirecting power.

761
00:34:13,136 --> 00:34:14,511
It will blow us up.

762
00:34:14,512 --> 00:34:16,513
- Kimi, send a K23
distress signal

763
00:34:16,514 --> 00:34:17,973
on the off chance someone
is around who can help us.

764
00:34:17,974 --> 00:34:19,350
- Eva, don't.
Don't, don't, don't.

765
00:34:19,351 --> 00:34:20,934
- Brice!
- Brice, what are you doing?

766
00:34:20,935 --> 00:34:22,102
- I'm sorry, mate.
- Have you lost your mind?

767
00:34:22,103 --> 00:34:23,812
- Yeah. Maybe. I think so.

768
00:34:23,813 --> 00:34:25,939
- K23 sent.

769
00:34:25,940 --> 00:34:28,484
- Uh, guys,
the core's overloading.

770
00:34:28,485 --> 00:34:29,985
- What?
- Look.

771
00:34:29,986 --> 00:34:31,321
The debris has disappeared.

772
00:34:48,963 --> 00:34:51,715
<i>[tense music]</i>

773
00:34:51,716 --> 00:34:53,967
- What the hell happened here?

774
00:34:53,968 --> 00:34:56,720
- The distress signal is
coming from the debris.

775
00:34:56,721 --> 00:34:58,722
It was an Ark.

776
00:34:58,723 --> 00:35:00,724
- Oh, God.

777
00:35:00,725 --> 00:35:03,185
- Watch out.
- Huh?

778
00:35:03,186 --> 00:35:04,520
Oh!
- Oh! Sorry.

779
00:35:04,521 --> 00:35:05,604
- Dr. Kabir.

780
00:35:05,605 --> 00:35:07,481
both: Listen, is Eva okay?

781
00:35:07,482 --> 00:35:09,400
both: As far as I know,
she's fine.

782
00:35:09,401 --> 00:35:12,569
But how are you doing?
Are you okay?

783
00:35:12,570 --> 00:35:15,614
both: Yeah, I'm fine.

784
00:35:15,615 --> 00:35:17,533
- How'd you know
what we were gonna say?

785
00:35:17,534 --> 00:35:21,245
- I keep telling you,
we are in a time loop.

786
00:35:21,246 --> 00:35:22,496
- Oh, God, which one?

787
00:35:22,497 --> 00:35:24,498
- Can't tell.
I can't find any identifier.

788
00:35:24,499 --> 00:35:27,876
- Captain, we're being dragged
by a very strong gravity force.

789
00:35:27,877 --> 00:35:30,421
- We dropped out of FTL
right next to the gravity well.

790
00:35:30,422 --> 00:35:31,797
Not sure of its source,
but it seems like

791
00:35:31,798 --> 00:35:33,132
some form of dark matter.

792
00:35:33,133 --> 00:35:34,633
- Might I suggest
we hightail it out of here

793
00:35:34,634 --> 00:35:36,468
before whatever did this
to that Ark does it to us?

794
00:35:36,469 --> 00:35:37,970
- Agreed.
Get us out of here, Kimi.

795
00:35:37,971 --> 00:35:39,263
Set a course for Homebase.

796
00:35:39,264 --> 00:35:40,889
- Did we ever try to stop
Kimi from doing that?

797
00:35:40,890 --> 00:35:42,808
[screeching]

798
00:35:42,809 --> 00:35:44,184
- That doesn't sound good.

799
00:35:44,185 --> 00:35:45,728
- Yeah, you stopped her.
Didn't help.

800
00:35:45,729 --> 00:35:47,688
- Strong gravity waves
are bending space-time.

801
00:35:47,689 --> 00:35:49,690
It's interfering with the FTL
forming a bubble.

802
00:35:49,691 --> 00:35:51,108
- Kimi, try the NEPS engines.

803
00:35:51,109 --> 00:35:52,276
- What about trying
to stop that?

804
00:35:52,277 --> 00:35:53,319
- Yeah.

805
00:35:53,320 --> 00:35:54,278
- Brice, who are you
talking to?

806
00:35:54,279 --> 00:35:55,446
- Just--
- The gravity pull

807
00:35:55,447 --> 00:35:56,989
is too strong.
- Damn it. I can redirect

808
00:35:56,990 --> 00:35:59,033
non-life support systems
to goose the NEPS.

809
00:35:59,034 --> 00:36:00,534
Maybe it'll be enough
to move us out far.

810
00:36:00,535 --> 00:36:02,494
- Do it.
- Have we tried stopping that?

811
00:36:02,495 --> 00:36:05,247
- We've tried stopping
everything they do.

812
00:36:05,248 --> 00:36:06,999
[computer chirps]
- Overload.

813
00:36:07,000 --> 00:36:08,375
- There has to be
something else we can try.

814
00:36:08,376 --> 00:36:09,585
- There's gotta be something
that we haven't tried.

815
00:36:09,586 --> 00:36:11,587
- I don't think there is.
- We've tried everything.

816
00:36:11,588 --> 00:36:14,256
- Kimi?
- I got nothing. I'm sorry.

817
00:36:14,257 --> 00:36:15,632
- I think we're stranded here.

818
00:36:15,633 --> 00:36:17,968
- Eva, try redirecting power
from the FTL chargers.

819
00:36:17,969 --> 00:36:19,386
- Yeah, stopped that too.

820
00:36:19,387 --> 00:36:20,846
- Kimi, send a K23
distress signal

821
00:36:20,847 --> 00:36:23,140
on the off chance someone
is around who can help us.

822
00:36:23,141 --> 00:36:24,433
- Wait, wait, wait,
wait, wait, wait.

823
00:36:24,434 --> 00:36:26,018
We answered that distress call.

824
00:36:26,019 --> 00:36:27,352
- Yes.

825
00:36:27,353 --> 00:36:29,063
- So maybe
we can prevent ourselves

826
00:36:29,064 --> 00:36:30,356
ever coming here
in the first place.

827
00:36:30,357 --> 00:36:32,316
- Yes!

828
00:36:32,317 --> 00:36:34,151
- Kimi, belay that order.
Don't send that signal.

829
00:36:34,152 --> 00:36:36,236
- Whoa! Why, Brice?

830
00:36:36,237 --> 00:36:39,490
- Because that debris
out there, that's us.

831
00:36:39,491 --> 00:36:42,660
<i>♪ ♪</i>

832
00:36:42,661 --> 00:36:43,702
- What?

833
00:36:43,703 --> 00:36:45,788
- Should I send?

834
00:36:45,789 --> 00:36:46,914
- Look.

835
00:36:46,915 --> 00:36:48,291
The bloody debris disappeared.

836
00:36:51,252 --> 00:36:53,962
- Don't send, Kimi.

837
00:36:53,963 --> 00:36:55,339
- Starting the NEPS now.

838
00:36:55,340 --> 00:36:58,676
[computer beeps, NEPS whirs]

839
00:36:58,677 --> 00:37:01,387
<i>♪ ♪</i>

840
00:37:01,388 --> 00:37:05,225
Captain,
the NEPS is overloaded.

841
00:37:24,536 --> 00:37:27,204
- Can't let anyone know
about this.

842
00:37:27,205 --> 00:37:28,872
- Know about what?

843
00:37:28,873 --> 00:37:30,749
- This.

844
00:37:30,750 --> 00:37:33,711
<i>[soft music]</i>

845
00:37:33,712 --> 00:37:37,881
<i>♪ ♪</i>

846
00:37:37,882 --> 00:37:40,009
- You know, I just had
the weirdest déjà vu.

847
00:37:40,010 --> 00:37:41,760
- [laughs]

848
00:37:41,761 --> 00:37:43,220
Shut up.

849
00:37:43,221 --> 00:37:50,353
<i>♪ ♪</i>

850
00:38:14,044 --> 00:38:17,004
<i>[suspenseful music]</i>

851
00:38:17,005 --> 00:38:21,675
<i>♪ ♪</i>

852
00:38:21,676 --> 00:38:23,094
- Hey, what's up?

853
00:38:31,227 --> 00:38:33,187
- That took you long enough.

854
00:38:33,188 --> 00:38:40,153
<i>♪ ♪</i>

855
00:38:56,252 --> 00:38:57,252
- Report.

856
00:38:57,253 --> 00:38:58,671
- Still on course.

857
00:38:58,672 --> 00:39:00,672
- All systems are normal.

858
00:39:00,673 --> 00:39:03,217
- Maintain course for Homebase.

859
00:39:03,218 --> 00:39:06,136
<i>[gentle music]</i>

860
00:39:06,137 --> 00:39:13,144
<i>♪ ♪</i>

861
00:39:21,987 --> 00:39:24,071
- Did you just do something
to me?

862
00:39:24,072 --> 00:39:25,280
- No. Why?

863
00:39:25,281 --> 00:39:26,657
- Because I feel like
I've been doing

864
00:39:26,658 --> 00:39:28,701
laps around the ship
or something.

865
00:39:28,702 --> 00:39:31,662
- Then lay down, get some rest.

866
00:39:31,663 --> 00:39:33,288
- You did, didn't you?

867
00:39:33,289 --> 00:39:34,915
You did something to me.

868
00:39:34,916 --> 00:39:38,544
- Told you, I can't
control you like that.

869
00:39:38,545 --> 00:39:41,505
- Then why am I so tired?

870
00:39:41,506 --> 00:39:43,215
- Don't know.

871
00:39:43,216 --> 00:39:44,508
Easy solution?

872
00:39:44,509 --> 00:39:45,968
Go to sleep.

873
00:39:45,969 --> 00:39:48,637
- And what are you gonna do?

874
00:39:48,638 --> 00:39:50,973
- What do you want me to do?

875
00:39:50,974 --> 00:39:52,766
- Honestly?

876
00:39:52,767 --> 00:39:54,894
I want you to leave me alone.

877
00:39:58,606 --> 00:40:00,399
[grunts]

878
00:40:00,400 --> 00:40:03,319
<i>[tense music]</i>

879
00:40:03,320 --> 00:40:08,115
<i>♪ ♪</i>

880
00:40:08,116 --> 00:40:09,868
[sighs]

881
00:40:22,338 --> 00:40:25,799
- What do you want
the team to do now?

882
00:40:25,800 --> 00:40:29,595
- You should just hold
their heels for now.

883
00:40:29,596 --> 00:40:32,766
I'll make a move
when the time is right.

884
00:40:37,228 --> 00:40:39,021
- I don't hear anything.

885
00:40:39,022 --> 00:40:42,274
- Would you mind
turning on your implants?

886
00:40:42,275 --> 00:40:43,692
[device beeps]

887
00:40:43,693 --> 00:40:45,862
[electric pulsing]

888
00:40:48,406 --> 00:40:50,282
[screeching]

889
00:40:50,283 --> 00:40:54,703
- [screaming]

890
00:40:54,704 --> 00:40:56,580
Turn if off!

891
00:40:56,581 --> 00:40:57,790
[screams]

892
00:40:57,791 --> 00:40:59,124
- I can't turn it off.

893
00:40:59,125 --> 00:41:01,210
- I'm gonna move the slab
away from her.

894
00:41:01,211 --> 00:41:02,586
[yells]

895
00:41:02,587 --> 00:41:05,089
Ah!
- Angus!

896
00:41:05,090 --> 00:41:06,340
- I'm okay.

897
00:41:06,341 --> 00:41:07,758
[grunts]
I'm okay.

898
00:41:07,759 --> 00:41:10,887
- [screaming]

899
00:41:12,055 --> 00:41:15,266
[screeching stops]
[panting]

900
00:41:19,312 --> 00:41:20,771
- Kelly?

901
00:41:20,772 --> 00:41:23,941
Are--are you all right?

902
00:41:23,942 --> 00:41:25,567
- Yeah, I think so.

903
00:41:25,568 --> 00:41:27,027
- Guys.

904
00:41:27,028 --> 00:41:29,863
Guys, you have to see this.

905
00:41:29,864 --> 00:41:32,116
- Hey, come on.

906
00:41:32,117 --> 00:41:33,909
- [grunts]

907
00:41:33,910 --> 00:41:36,787
<i>[suspenseful music]</i>

908
00:41:36,788 --> 00:41:39,623
<i>♪ ♪</i>

909
00:41:39,624 --> 00:41:41,083
- It's in English.

910
00:41:41,084 --> 00:41:48,091
<i>♪ ♪</i>

911
00:42:00,019 --> 00:42:03,147
<i>[soft dramatic music]</i>

912
00:42:03,148 --> 00:42:10,113
<i>♪ ♪</i>

